body:
Custom elements have been working great for me, but one improvement I kept thinking about was about the relation of the custom element name and its definition. For example, given the following definition:

```js
// my-element.js
export class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
}
customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);
```

When you `import './my-element.js',` you implicitly rely on the side-effect of the custom element definition to properly show up in your document. An alternative approach that @paullewis and I have been exploring is to only `export` classes and then have 1 file with all the definition calls. E.g.

```js
// my-element.js
export class MyElement extends HTMLElement {}
```
```js
// my-other-element.js
export class MyOtherElement extends HTMLElement {}
```
```js
// definitions.js
import { MyElement } from './my-element.js';
import { MyOtherElement } from './my-other-element.js';

customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);
customElements.define('my-other-element', MyOtherElement);
```

I like this pattern, because it makes figuring out the answer to the question "What are the names given to all my Element definitions?" really easy. It also makes importing the individual elements side-effect free. This helps with testing, where sometimes you want to define elements with a different name to test behavior of progressive enhancement.

However, the big downside remains: there is a "magic" relation (to be clear: by design of course!) between the name and the definition. This means that if you are refactoring code that relies on the relation, it becomes more of a "ctrl + F through the project and replace all strings" job rather than letting us the powerful tooling in our editors.

This led me to the following idea: what if you can pass in the custom element class reference into `document.createElement`. Given the definitions from above and the following document:

```html
<script type="module" src="definitions.js"></script>
<body>
  <div id="container"></div>
  <script type="module">
    import { MyElement} from './my-element.js';

   document.getElementById('container').appendChild(document.createElement(MyElement));
  </script>
</body>
```

This would result in the following output:

```html
<div id="container">
  <my-element></my-element>
</div>
```

The end of this result would be that type-checkers and general variable resolution will suddenly discover that if you make a change to, for example, the class name the user code will need to be changed. This would thus allow for better tooling support and code maintainability.

If `document.createElement` is called with a class that is not (yet) registered as a custom element, it should throw.

P.S. Note that this idea explicitly disallows passing in built-in constructors (`HTMLDivElement`), as @jakearchibald pointed out to me that the `HTMLHeadingElement` would be problematic. E.g. you wouldn't know which version of the heading it would be. It could be that the proposal can be broadened to built-in elements as well, but I wanted to keep that out for now.
